#: glasgow.applet.interface.i2c_controller.I2CControllerInterface.probe:1 of
msgid "Run a probe sequence."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.applet.interface.i2c_controller.I2CControllerInterface.probe:3 of
msgid "Executes the :py:`sequence` against an I²C target at :py:`address`."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.applet.interface.i2c_controller.I2CControllerInterface.probe:7 of
msgid "This is an **inherently dangerous** action. If the device at :py:`address` does not conform to the assumptions used when designing :py:`sequence`, the outcome is unpredictable and may cause damage to the device and/or the assembly it is a part of."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.applet.interface.i2c_controller.I2CControllerInterface.probe:11 of
msgid "Returns :py:`True` if the sequence matches, :py:`False` otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:1 of
msgid "One step of a device probe sequence."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:3 of
msgid "Describes an action taken by an I²C controller taken to check whether a specific I²C target address matches the identity of a particular chip."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:6 of
msgid "While probe sequences can be created by constructing the underlying Python objects, it is recomended to use :meth:`parse` with a compact string representation. Not all probe sequences are well-formed; use :meth:`verify` to ensure that a sequence describes a legal I²C transaction."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:10 of
msgid "The string representation is a whitespace-separated sequence of the following tokens (chosen to resemble the diagrams in the I²C specification):"
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:13 of
msgid "``S``: START condition."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:14 of
msgid "``Sr``: repeated START condition."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:15 of
msgid "``P``: STOP condition."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:16 of
msgid "``AW``: WRITE target address."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:17 of
msgid "``AR``: READ target address."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:18 of
msgid "``0dDDD`` where ``DDD`` is a 1 to 3 digit decimal number: data to read or write, in decimal."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:19 of
msgid "``0xHH`` where ``H`` is a hex digit or ``?``: data to read or write, in hexadecimal."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:20 of
msgid "``0bBBBBBBBB`` where ``B`` is a binary digit or ``?``: data to read or write, in binary."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:22 of
msgid "The ``AW`` and ``AR`` tokens refer to the actual device address, which is not known until the moment when the probing is done; probe sequences are address-independent."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:25 of
msgid "Consider the following example probe sequences:"
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:27 of
msgid "``S AW 0xD0 Sr AR 0x60 P``: detects Bosch BME280 sensors by writing :py:`0xD0` to select the register, then reading the register value and comparing it with :py:`0x60`."
msgstr ""

#: glasgow.arch.i2c.ProbeStep:29 of
msgid "``S AW 117 Sr AR 0b?110100? P``: detects InvenSense MPU-60X0 by writing :py:`117` to select the register, then reading the register value and comparing it with :py:`0b01101000` while masking off (ignoring) the first and last bit."
msgstr ""

